This condition shows **high variability** across runs (PF ranging 0.57–0.82, modulus 151–215 bar). With 4 runs, the mean pore fraction is approximately 0.68 and mean modulus ~171 bar. The CV is moderate. This is a promising candidate but the variability is high — it has not been independently confirmed as a stable Pareto-front point.

**Validation status:** The 15.5/6°C/250s/N2/1600s condition has 4 runs in the 10 wt% bath, but with high variability (PF SD ~0.11, modulus SD ~28 bar across runs). This is enough data to estimate the mean but the spread is large. The condition is not yet confirmed as a reliable Pareto improvement.

**Exploration opportunity:** The data in the 10 wt% bath is concentrated around polymer_wt 14–15.5, bath_temp 6–10°C, pullcast_speed 5–8, coupon_to_bath_wait_time 200–350s. The **higher polymer concentration (16–17 wt%)** with the 10 wt% bath is undersampled — only one attempt at 16 wt% (which had a top-gap defect). Higher polymer concentration tends to increase modulus; the 10 wt% bath (which slows phase separation) combined with higher polymer_wt could yield a different modulus/pore-fraction tradeoff.

**Decision:** The 15.5/6°C/250s condition has shown the best combined performance in the 10 wt% bath but with high run-to-run variability. However, 4 runs have been completed. Rather than repeating indefinitely, I should now **explore a nearby but distinct region** that could improve the Pareto front: slightly higher polymer concentration (16 wt%) with the 10 wt% bath, moderate bath temp (7–8°C), and moderate wait time. This is under-sampled in the current bath context and physically motivated — higher polymer_wt should increase modulus while the 10 wt% bath may preserve pore fraction better than a pure water bath.

The 16 wt% / 9°C attempt had a top-gap defect. I'll try **16 wt%, bath_temp 7°C, pullcast_speed 6, coupon_to_bath_wait_time 250s, N2, nips_bath_wait_time 1600s, mixing_temp 50°C** — a modest step from the 15.5 wt% region toward higher modulus while keeping conditions that have produced good membranes.

