### Key Observations

**Best candidates in 10 wt% bath:**
- **15p5-0add-6deg-run2**: Modulus 162.8, Pore Fraction 0.73 — good quality, high pore fraction
- **15p5-0add-6deg-run3**: Modulus 215.0, Pore Fraction 0.57 — good quality, high modulus
- **15p5-0add-6deg-run4**: Modulus 151.1, Pore Fraction 0.82 — good quality, very high pore fraction

The condition **polymer_wt=15.5, bath_temp=6°C, pullcast_speed=6, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=250, nips_bath_wait_time=1600, mixing_temp=40, N2=true** has been run 4 times with the 10 wt% bath. Results show high variability: pore fraction ranges 0.57–0.82, modulus 151–215 bar. This is a promising region but with high run-to-run variability.

**Validation status**: The 15p5/6°C/250s condition has 4 independent runs (run1 had minor crease, runs 2–4 were good quality). The mean modulus across good-quality runs (2,3,4) is ~176 bar, mean pore fraction ~0.71. This is already reasonably validated with 3 clean runs, though variability is high (CV on pore fraction ~0.18).

**Under-explored region in 10 wt% bath**: Higher polymer concentrations (16–17 wt%) with the 10 wt% bath have barely been explored (only 16 wt% at 9°C and 7°C, both with top-gap defects). The 0 wt% bath data shows that higher polymer_wt (16–17) tends to give higher modulus. With the 10 wt% bath slowing phase separation, a moderate polymer concentration with a colder bath could yield both higher modulus and maintained pore fraction.

**Gap in coverage**: The combination of **polymer_wt ~15.5–16, bath_temp ~5–7°C, moderate wait time ~200–300s** with the 10 wt% bath is under-sampled. The 15p5/6°C condition is promising but we haven't tried slightly higher polymer_wt (16.0) with a very cold bath (5°C) in the 10 wt% bath context.

**Proposed experiment**: polymer_wt=16.0, bath_temp=5°C, mixing_temp=45°C, pullcast_speed=6, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=250s, nips_bath_wait_time=1600s, N2=true.

**Rationale**: 
- Higher polymer_wt (16.0 vs 15.5) should increase modulus (consistent with 0 wt% bath trends)
- Colder bath (5°C vs 6°C) slows phase separation further, potentially maintaining or increasing pore fraction
- The 10 wt% PolarClean bath already slows demixing; combining with cold bath and slightly higher polymer concentration explores a promising but unsampled region
- Moderate wait time (250s) and slow speed (6 mm/s) are consistent with the successful 15p5/6°C runs
- This is a genuinely new point in the 10 wt% bath space (no prior experiment at polymer_wt=16.0, bath_temp=5°C with this bath)
- Expected benefit: potentially higher modulus than 15p5 runs (~200+ bar) while maintaining pore fraction ≥0.60

